{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1470353-question-about-stone-of-scorn","id":1470353,"title":"Question about Stone of Scorn","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-12-13T10:40:18-08:00","id":1470353,"title":"Question about Stone of Scorn","content":"(Trigger warning: post deals with suicide.)<br /> <br /> So a PC was instrumental in an NPC garou's suicide. He was insensitive to their state of near-harano after the NPC lost his packmates, and moved forward on adopting a kinfolk associated with the NPC's pack who wanted out. The NPC's new alpha warned the PC to delay the adoption, but he went ahead and after several painful scenes filled with red flags the NPC killed himself. (This is a very crude summary of what was a much more nuanced series of scenes, and I've left out plenty of important details.)<br /> <br /> There's lots of blame to go round, but the Grand Elder's gonna hold a Stone of Scorn ritual for the PC involved to punish him for his arrogance and callousness. My question is this: are the other PC's expected to take part in the ritual and criticize their packmate, whose actions they also disapproved of?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26645-pwtucker","name":"pwtucker","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26645_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"68"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-13T12:17:00-08:00","id":1470361,"title":"","content":"Despite a lot of writing found in the Punishment Rites of the corebook and Justice in the Players Guides, there's just a lot of things an ST has to determine for themselves. As long as you give enough consideration to it, and come up with something that fits your interpretation of the setting and makes sense to your players, you'll probably do fine.<br /> <br /> And I will also emphasize that because justice is sept based, that different septs will interpret things differently based on tribal membership, the caern purpose, the type of caern totem, and so on. So there can be variety. A Children of Gaia sept at a Caern of Calm or Fellowship will handle things differently than a Get of Fenris sept at a Caern of Rage or a Fianna/Silver Fangs multitribal sept at a Caern of Justice.<br /> <br /> I think in general, most septs will have the expectations that all sept members will participate in most of the Punishment Rites of the sept. It would be important to demonstrate sept unity in this regard, and that the sept as a whole supports the punishment, and that it is not done by the whim of a small group. The description of the Stone of Scorn implies, though does not directly state, that it is the entire sept that participates. Certainly the inclusion of the offender's packmates as rite participants makes it clear that the offender's crime was real. Also, the more participants you have in the rite, the lower the difficulty of the rite. It would be embarrassing if a Punishment Rite failed, so septs would encourage both a highly skilled ritualist to lead the Rite as well as many participants as possible to lower the difficulty.<br /> <br /> There can be legitimate reasons for sept members to not attend a Punishment Rite. Lesser crimes dealt with by lesser rites might be more administrative in nature and not worth the entire sept's time. Certain packs might already be on quests that would be interrupted. Or there may be emergencies that keep someone from attending. But I don't think that would include pack members not wanting to be part of the punishment of a packmate. I think it would be very important that packmates participate in the rite in order to demonstrate the solidarity of the entire sept when it comes to punishment. In some septs, there may be political reasons why someone would recuse themselves and not attend, but I don't think that would be ideal as it would harm the concept that justice is communal and restorative of norms.<br /> <br /> So I believe the other PCs should participate in the Rite. Having said that, I think it would be fine to keep their satirical comments to a minimum. Keep it to the point of the offender's failure. Don't sugar coat or excuse it, clearly point out the error in their behavior. But don't use it as an opportunity to show how clever they can insult someone, or use it as an opportunity to get back at them for some slight. After all, they will still be packmates after this. Their scolding should be more like a parent than a schoolmate mocking someone to gain popularity.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-13T12:38:53-08:00","id":1470364,"title":"","content":"As always, Black Fox, your comments are on point. Thanks for confirming what I was suspecting. This is a Shadow Lord sept dedicated to Wisdom under Owl, so there's a decidedly darker tone to the proceedings, with an eye kept out for long term benefits and fairness.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26645-pwtucker","name":"pwtucker","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26645_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"